[Music]  talent is everywhere but at many of  america's top institutions black leaders  are still  few and far between that's the  conclusion of cbs news political  contributor jamal simmons  who reviewed publicly available data on  diversity at several u.s companies and  universities  simmons found the percentage of black  people in leadership jobs is often stuck  around four percent that's even though  13 percent of americans are black  apple amazon google and facebook report  their diversity numbers publicly every  year  and so we can see what simmons is  talking about in 2020  only four percent of apple's leaders  were black amazon facebook and google  were all hovering around 3 percent the 4  percent phenomena as simmons calls it is  a pattern  he also found among tenured faculty at  some of the most elite schools in  america that includes  harvard yale duke mit and the university  of california  at berkeley and as jamal puts it quote  it's as if someone has put  sticky tar paper on the road to african  american  inclusion and we should also point out  that these companies and universities  have  almost without variation made public and  significant investments and commitments  to narrowing the gap and promoting  diversity  and inclusion on a broad scale so let's  bring in jamal simmons now to talk about  all this jamal good morning to you um  the article is in a new journal or the  new article is in a journal called  democracy  uh and you make the point in it that  these  goodwill efforts by companies while  sincere have not so far  been very effective so what needs to be  done yeah you know tony um 25 years ago  i was a graduate student at harvard and  we had a racial incident on campus and  so we  confronted the dean of the kennedy  school at the time about why there were  no african-american tenured faculty  at the kennedy school he said for the  most part  it's a pipeline problem there aren't  enough people coming through the  pipeline well that was 25 years ago  there were none you know how many they  are today there are two  two out of 55 that's less than four  percent of the faculty at that school  and that's true for harvard-wide it's  true for yale as you went through  so i began you know as as george floyd  happened and brianna taylor and company  started making a lot of these statements  about commitment to  racial equality and dealing with  systemic racism i began to wonder do the  companies and organizations have the  teams  at their fingertips that can actually  deal with this very significant problem  and the more research i did the more i  kept finding that those teams are coming  up short and here's the thing  it's not just about one particular  company that's what was so amazing it's  about a whole sector  it's all about a trend that runs across  sectors i'll give you one story and we  can go to the next  colin powell was chairman of the joint  chiefs of staff  25 years ago we are all very honored by  that he was the first african-american  chairman  today 25 years later not another black  chairman of the joint chiefs of staff  there are 41 of the most senior  commanders in the military who can even  be considered only two of them are  african-american it's about 4.9  so if you think about this like a  parking lot and you number the spaces  one through  ten most of the cars would be clustered  around space number four  and so it's it's not a ceiling it's just  sort of a cluster around four percent  and why  is it we can't really break through that  yeah the four percent phenomena one of  the points you make in the article which  i thought was compelling is that these  companies and the military all these  institutions  they look left and they look right and  they say well we're doing as well as the  other guys so i guess we're doing okay  but that's a false sense of comfort in  terms of their progress so how do they  push beyond that what needs to happen  you know melody hobson's been here as a  matter of fact she was talking to you  guys  back in february and she talked about  this when she became chair of the  starbucks board we got to attack this  problem from both ends of the pipeline  you got to deal with the people who are  coming into the pipeline at the entry  level  and make sure we have recruitment  recruitment criteria that allow more  people to come in  and then we've got to deal with people  at the top levels in the leadership  and what i find is most leaders many  leaders have come through black-led  organizations and so if we think about  building formal partnerships formal  relationships  through many of the organizations  whether they're historically black  colleges or people like melody hopson  right who got hired out of princeton by  john rogers she worked at aerial  investments for years  she grew there she flowered there she  became her own person there  and then she moved into board leadership  at other places right  we see that happen over and over again  darren walker who's at the  ford foundation who's the president he  was at the abyssinian development  corporation before he went into you know  rockefeller and then ford  we see this trend line i say let's  formalize it let's  build some of these relationships so we  don't [ __ ] those black-led  organizations when we cherry-pick their  talent  and let's bring some folks into  mainstream at the top so the candidates  coming up through the pipeline  have role models and people who are  building a culture that will enable  their success so jamal we all agree that  four percent number  ain't so good what would progress look  like to you what is the number that you  go okay  now we're doing now we're talking so  tony brought it up in the beginning so  13  of the country is african-american 10 of  college graduates are african-american  it just seemed so now we're at four  percent in terms of leadership  it just seems like we ought to be  getting closer into the eight nine ten  percent  uh range if we're gonna have parity and  one of the things as  tony just mentioned that we found i  found in the research talking to a lot  of people who  who do this work is you've got to set  clear goals about what you want  and then you've got to really lean on  policing what it is that you want some  great  professors did this thing about women on  boards you'll appreciate this  they found that uh that for a long time  having one woman on a board was  considered to be appropriate people  didn't think so  so all the companies looked around and  now you see a greater likelihood that  there are two women on the board so they  went from tokenism  to toucanism and they predict that at  some point they'll be threakanism  right because people will decide two is  not enough the same thing exists with  racial  racial diversity at the leadership level  jamal we're running out of time talent  is everywhere  opportunity should be as well thank you  very much you're watching cbs this  morning we'll be right back

